// The pet: a sitting cat, as vector. Replaced the floppy-eared dog
// 2026-09-23 at the user's request; moods, colours and the split are
// unchanged.
//
// Each shape is drawn twice under a left/right clip so each half takes a
// partner's colour -- the same "crossover split" technique
// CrossoverHeart.tsx uses for the app icon's motif: day-orange is your
// partner, night-blue is you.
//
// SYMMETRY ABOUT x=50 IS LOAD-BEARING, not tidiness. The shapes are clipped
// into halves at exactly that line, so any asymmetry hands one partner a
// visibly larger half. Every path was generated by writing only the right
// half and reflecting it (x -> 100-x, with the curve chain walked backwards
// so control points swap), which is why every coordinate has an exact
// partner. petPaths.test.ts enforces it rather than trusting it. No tail for
// the same reason: a tail curls to one side.
//
// THE SILHOUETTE NEVER CHANGES BETWEEN MOODS -- only the eyes and mouth
// move. A mood that needed a different silhouette wouldn't be a mood, it'd
// be a second creature.
//
// Two layers rather than one outline: the body (with its two paws as
// separate closed subpaths) is drawn first, then the head over it. One
// combined path would stroke the body's top edge straight across the face.

